          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Broadly speaking,
            %strong constructive mathematics
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            done without the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+excluded+middle"} principle of excluded middle
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+proof"} formal proof
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+by+contradiction"} proof by contradiction
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
          %p
            There are variations of what exactly is regarded as constructive mathematics, for instance
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+mathematics"} intuitionism
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicativism
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#OriginsAndSchools"} below
            = succeed "-style" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brouwer"} Brouwer
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+mathematics"} intuitionistic mathematics
            one includes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            that
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contradiction"} contradict
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} choice
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theorists
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative"} predicative
            constructive and “intuitionistic” to mean impredicative constructive. Other authors emphasize the necessity that constructive theories be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+relevance"} proof relevant
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Harper13"} Harper 2013
          %p
            %strong Constructivism
            is the philosophy that such mathematics is useful, or (more strongly) that non-constructive mathematics is wrong. Historically, constructive mathematics was first pursued explicitly by mathematicians who believed the latter. However, many modern mathematicians who do constructive mathematics do it not because of any philosophical belief about the wrongness of non-constructive mathematics, but because constructive mathematics is interesting in its own right. In the ‘
            %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.93.9892"}> pluralist
            ’ approach to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            of mathematics, a constructive proof (when it exists) is better because it is valid in more versions of mathematics, but a classical proof remains valid for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
          %p
            Another motivation for modern mathematicians —especially category theorists like those on the nLab— is that the study of constructive mathematics has potential applications to non-constructive mathematics. For example, even if one believes the principle of excluded middle to be true, the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"}> internal
            ” version of excluded middle in many interesting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} categories
            is still false; thus constructive mathematics can be useful in the study of such categories, even if mathematics is “globally” non-constructive. This is the neutral motivation for constructive mathematics from the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV#Logic"} nPOV
          %p Here we write mostly about the mathematics, trying to be mostly neutral philosophically.
          %h2#OriginsAndSchools Origins and schools
          %p
            During the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundational
            crisis” in mathematics around the beginning of the 20th century, a number of mathematicians espoused philosophies that are generally grouped together and labeled
            = succeed "." do
              %strong constructivist
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
                and
            %li
              %p
                the principle of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
          %p
            Thus, constructivists (including many still active today) reject proofs that make use of either of these. (In fact, it was realized in 1975 by Diaconescu that the axiom of choice implies the principle of excluded middle. Excluded middle is precisely the “finitely indexed” axiom of choice; see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            for a proof.)
          %p There are, however, differences among constructivists as well, even discounting the pluralists.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Some, like
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Richman"} Fred Richman
                (see (Richman 2000)), simply remove choice and excluded middle from
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
                with nothing to replace them. This is called
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutral+constructive+mathematics"} neutral constructive mathematics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
                to itself, without using
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            %li
              %p
                Others accept weaker versions of choice, such as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
                or even
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+choice"} dependent choice
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toby+Bartels"} Toby Bartels
                argues that the intuition behind accepting these justifies the (yet stronger)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentation+axiom"} presentation axiom
                (that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the+category+of+sets"} the category of sets
                has
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enough+projectives"} enough projectives
            %li
              %p
                Others add “non-classical” axioms which contradict choice or excluded middle, but which are consistent in their absence, such as “all total functions
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1] \to \mathbb{R}
                are continuous” (the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuity+axiom"} continuity axiom
                of the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionism"}> intuitionistic
                ” school of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L.+E.+J.+Brouwer"} L. E. J. Brouwer
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
                are computable” (the
                %span.newWikiWord
                  computability axiom
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/computability+axiom"} ?
                of the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russian+constructivism"}> Russian
                ” school of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrey+Markov+Jr"} A. A. Markov
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Errett+Bishop"} Errett Bishop
                had his own branch of constructive mathematics, known as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bishop%27s+constructive+mathematics"} Bishop's constructive mathematics
                or
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BISH"} BISH
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
                are defined notion rather than a primitive on
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            %li
              %p
                Still others, following
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Weyl"} Hermann Weyl
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative mathematics
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
                of constructive mathematics (in particular, in those of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Aczel"} Aczel
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-L%C3%B6f"} Martin-Löf
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Harvey+Friedman"} Friedman
                or
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"} Coquand
            %li
              %p
                An extreme form of constructive
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative mathematics
                is to reject the use of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
                and the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantifier"} universal quantifier
                completely and work in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+logic"} coherent logic
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+mathematics"} coherent mathematics
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent"} sequent
                rather than a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formula"} formula
                in the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+logic"} coherent logic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+mathematics"} coherent mathematics
                include
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
                which does not make use of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+types"} function types
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+function+types"} dependent function types
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+pretopos"} arithmetic pretopos
            %li
              %p
                One also has
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+mathematics"} geometric mathematics
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
                but no
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+sets"} function sets
            %li
              %p
                Very extreme constructivists, like
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Doron+Zeilberger"} Doron Zeilberger
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitism"} finitism
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} in
                the category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
                of
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+sets"} finite sets
            %li
              %p
                The most extreme position of all is to deny even the existence of very large finite sets. This is called
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafinitism"} ultrafinitism
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Alexander Yesenin-Volpin
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Alexander+Yesenin-Volpin"} ?
                and (more recently)
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Nelson"} Edward Nelson
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soft+linear+logic"} soft linear logic
                can also be argued to have an ultrafinitist flavor. Zeilberger also claims sympathy with ultrafinitist philosophy, although his actual work fits well into a (merely) finitist framework.
          %p
            Many constructivists (like many classical mathematicians) believe in an absolute mathematical sense of “truth,” and that in this sense choice and excluded middle are simply
            = succeed "." do
              %em wrong
            %a{:href => "#truevassert"} Truth versus assertability
            below.) To most mathematicians, this makes them seem quite strange. Other constructivists adopt a wait-and-see attitude, or even a relative notion of truth (which can seem strange in another way).
          %p The following is a partial list of schools and subtheories of constructive mathematics:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutral+constructive+mathematics"} neutral constructive mathematics
            %li
              %p
                Brouwer’s
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+mathematics"} intuitionistic mathematics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russian+constructivism"} Russian constructivism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bishop%27s+constructive+mathematics"} Bishop's constructive mathematics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+reverse+mathematics"} constructive reverse mathematics
            %li
              %p
                (at least related)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/explicit+mathematics"} explicit mathematics
          %h2#topos_theory Topos theory
          %p
            With the invention of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos theory
            in the second half of the 20th century, a new sort of constructivism arose. It was observed (by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"} Lawvere
            and others) that any topos with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            has an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            which is powerful enough to interpret most of mathematics, but that this logic in general fails to satisfy choice and excluded middle. This meant that even for a mathematican who likes to use choice and excluded middle (and
            %em a fortiori
            for one who believes them to be “true”), there is a reason to care about what can be proven without them, because only if a proof is constructive can it be interpreted in an arbitrary topos with NNO.
          %p
            Even starting from a “completely classical” world of mathematics, many interesting toposes arise naturally (such as the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaves
            on any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one-sided+real+number"} one-sided real number
            that is both a lower real and an upper real must be located (which, classically, is an utter triviality) becomes the theorem that any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicontinuous+function"} semicontinuous function
            (from any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            to the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous
            (which is at least somewhat nontrivial).
          %p
            By now it is known that many of the non-classical axioms used by the early constructivists have natural models in particular toposes. For instance, in the topos of sheaves on the real numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuity+axiom"} continuity axiom
            that “all total functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \to R
            are continuous” holds. And in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+topos"} effective topos
            %span.newWikiWord
              computability axiom
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/computability+axiom"} ?
            that “all partial functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N\to N
            are computable” holds.
          %p
            However, there are no non-classical (or classical) axioms beyond “pure constructivism” that are true in
            %em all
            toposes with NNO. In particular, there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+topos"} free topos
            with NNO such that a statement is true in the free topos precisely when it is provable in pure (Richman-school) constructive mathematics. This means that for an argument to apply in all toposes, even mild assumptions such as countable or dependent choice are unacceptable (but impredicativity is fine). However, topos theory has also provided ideas that solve many of the problems with pure constructivism. For example, a well-behaved notion of “continuous function” can be recovered by using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locales
            instead of topological spaces, which was first discovered in the context of toposes and is closely related to them in any case.
          %h2#some_features_of_constructive_mathematics Some features of constructive mathematics
          %h3#rephrasing_of_classical_ideas Rephrasing of classical ideas
          %p
            Sometimes, all that is necessary to make a piece of mathematics constructive is careful use of language. It is common in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            to define things with an unnecessary amount of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negations"} double negations
          %p
            For example, classically one often speaks about “nonempty” sets, meaning a set which “does
            %em not
            have
            %em no
            elements.” Constructively it is much better to say that a set “
            %em does
            have at least one element”; constructivists often call such a set
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
            to remind themselves that it is a “positive” notion to replace the negative one of “nonempty”. Others continue to use the word “non-empty” but understand it as a term of art that really means “inhabited”.
          %h3#bifurcation_of_notions Bifurcation of notions
          %p
            On the other hand, differences in axiomatization or definition that make no difference classically can result in actual differences in behavior constructively. Therefore, classically equivalent notions often “bifurcate” (or “trifurcate” or worse) into multiple inequivalent constructive ones. This tends to happen whenever a concept involves
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
            and, to a lesser degree,
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunction"} disjunction
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantification"} existential quantification
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                There are multiple inequivalent notions of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/denial+inequality"} denial inequality
                does not have the same properties in constructive mathematics that it does in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+equality"} stable equality
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable+equality"} decidable equality
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tight+apartness+relations"} tight apartness relations
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+sets"} classical sets
            %li
              %p
                There are multiple inequivalent constructive definitions of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
                %em nonzero
                element has an inverse” and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ≠
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \neq 1
            %li
              %p
                More generally, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antithesis+interpretation"} antithesis interpretation
                of constructive mathematics leads to a generalised notion of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
                as two
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mutually+exclusive+propositions"} mutually exclusive propositions
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-set"}>
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
                  \-set
                ),
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-monoid"}>
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
                  \-monoid
                ),
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-group"}>
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
                  \-group
                ),
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-ring"}>
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
                  \-ring
                ), and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antithesis+partial+order"}>
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
                  \-partial order
                ) into at least five concepts each, depending on the relation between the pointwise mutually exclusive relations and their intuitionistic
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/denial+negation"} denial negation
            %li
              %p
                There are multiple inequivalent definitions of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              %ul
                %li
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
                  and the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+real+numbers"} Cauchy real numbers
                  need no longer coincide. The Cauchy reals sit inside the Dedekind reals, but in general not every Dedekind real need be approximable by a
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
                  of rationals. From a topos-theoretic viewpoint, the Dedekind reals are usually the “correct” notion to study (if not the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale+of+real+numbers"} locale of real numbers
                  as a whole). However, the weak
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limited+principle+of+omniscience"} limited principle of omniscience
                  suffices to ensure that every Dedekind real is a Cauchy real, and hence that the two notions coincide; see (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Univalent+Foundations+Project"} Univalent Foundations Project
                  2013) and (Booij 2020). Weak countable choice implies the weak limited principle of omniscience, which in turn is implied by excluded middle and the axiom of choice; see (Bridges et al. 1993).
                %li
                  Similarly, the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+real+numbers"} Cauchy real numbers
                  are not sequentially
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+complete"} Cauchy complete
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
                  between the Cauchy reals and the Dedekind reals that are sequentially Cauchy complete called the
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+book+real+numbers"} HoTT book real numbers
                %li
                  The lower, upper, and two-sided
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
                  do not coincide with each other. That they do coincide is equivalent to
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
                  %a{:href => "https://categorytheory.zulipchat.com/#narrow/stream/229136-theory.3A-category-theory/topic/One.20universe.20as.20a.20foundation.20.26.20friends/near/465219817"} here
                  by
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Hanson"} James Hanson
                %li
                  A real number with a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locator"} locator
                  is equivalent to a real number represented by a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+sequence"} Cauchy sequence
                  with
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulus+of+convergence"} modulus of convergence
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequentially+complete"} sequentially complete
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulated+Cauchy+real+number"} modulated Cauchy real number
                  has a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locator"} locator
                  or is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+sequence"} Cauchy sequence
                  with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulus+of+convergence"} modulus of convergence
                  implies that the Cauchy real numbers coincide with the HoTT real numbers.
                %li
                  That every real number with a locator in the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+interval"} unit interval
                  can be represented by a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
                  of (positive) digits (i.e.
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radix+notation"} radix notation
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limited+principle+of+omniscience"} limited principle of omniscience
            %li
              %p
                There are at least three different constructive notions of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} ordinal number
            %li
              %p
                Without the axiom of choice,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functors
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor"} anafunctors
                become distinct, and often the latter is more appropriate; see (Makkai, 1996).
            %li
              %p
                Perhaps most disturbingly of all to the classical mathematician, one must distinguish between
                = succeed "," do
                  %em finite sets
                = succeed "," do
                  %em subfinite sets
                = succeed "," do
                  %em finitely-indexed sets
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em subfinitely indexed sets
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
                for definitions. However, in practice it is usually either finite or finitely-indexed sets that are important, and with practice a little bit of thought suffices to show which is the relevant concept.
          %h3#negative_translation Negative translation
          %p
            This allows one to translate classically valid theorems into intuitionistically valid theorems. See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation+translation"} double negation translation
          %h3#truevassert Truth versus assertability
          %p
            Already in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} provable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            because you believe it correct, then presumably you believe that ZFC is consistent even though (assuming that you're aware of certain theorems) you also know that you cannot prove it so. In that case, you also believe that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+hypothesis"} continuum hypothesis
            (for example) is either true or false; you may or may not have an opinion on which it is, but in any case again you know that you cannot prove it either way.
          %p
            A constructive mathematician can be even subtler. If you belong to the Bishop school, then you accept no classically false axioms, and anything that you can prove is valid also in classical mathematics. Even so, you can believe that the principle of excluded middle is false (even though, of course, you know that you cannot prove it false). So you can really confuse the classical mathematicians by saying, on the one hand, that they can safely accept all of your theorems as valid, and then saying, on the other hand, that you know excluded middle to be false. The resolution, of course, is that you never claimed that this was a
            = succeed "." do
              %em theorem
          %p
            This way of talking has even been formalised in (Bishop, 1967) with a convention adopted by many (but not all) of his followers. In this convention, the word ‘not’, used normally in a vernacular sentence whose mathematical content would otherwise be the proposition
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg p
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo →
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \to \bot
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            = succeed ").)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            = succeed "\u2019," do
              %i not
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo →
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \to x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is some statement that is known (although not proved!), according to Bishop, to be false. Now a statement that is ‘
            %i> not
            ’ true may be false, but this may be unknown, and it is even possible that it is also ‘
            %i> not
            ’ false (so
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi p
                    %mo →
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg p \to x
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            Bishop gives in his introduction several statements, suitable for use as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            above, including:
          %ul
            %li excluded middle itself, which Bishop call the ‘principle of omniscience’;
            %li
              the ‘
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limited+principle+of+omniscience"}> limited principle of omniscience
              ’: any infinite sequence in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}^N
              is either all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              or has at least one
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %li
              the ‘
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lesser+limited+principle+of+omniscience"}> lesser limited principle of omniscience
              ’: for any two infinite sequence in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}^N
              that do not both have at least one
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %li others.
          %p
            At one point in his book, while discussing the possibility of a pointwise-continuous function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1] \to R
            that is not uniformly continuous (a
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Specker function
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Specker+function"} ?
            %i not
            true and
            %i not
            false; he does not put it this way, so this may not be exactly what he meant, but there is no contradiction if it is. (But it
            %em is
            a contradiction, even in intuitionistic logic, if a statment is both not true and not false; indeed, a definition of ‘false’ may be taken to be ‘not true’.)
          %p
            This practice can be understood through a careful distinction between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+language"} object language
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metalanguage"} metalanguage
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            (such as the continuum hypothesis, or that a Specker function exists) belongs to the object language, as does
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg p
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \to x
            for any specific mathematical statement
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is provable in some formal system belongs to the metalanguage (although it can also phrased internal to any object language suitable for mathematics), and as such may be written
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \vdash p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash \neg p
            are different; the first claims that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            cannot be proved, while the second claims that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            can be refuted, which (in any
            %span.newWikiWord
              consistent
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/consistent+logic"} ?
            formal system, even a classical one) is strictly stronger. Although Bishop did not commit to any formal system, if we assume for the sake of argument that he settled on one, then we may write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash \neg p
            as our interpretation of his meaning when he asserts ‘not
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \vdash p
            as his meaning when he asserts ‘
            %i not
            = succeed "\u2019." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            Even without the notational convention of the Bishop school, it is important when reading constructive mathematics to remember the difference between what can be refuted (proved false) and what merely cannot be proved true. Although Brouwer's and Markov's schools will sometimes claim to prove statements that are (classically) outright false (such as that every function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1] \to R
            is pointwise-continuous), it is more common (and can happen in any school) that a constructive mathematician makes a claim that merely
            %em sounds
            false, when what they really mean is only that they don't accept what you say as true. Often modal phrases like ‘not necessarily’ will be used where Bishop would use ‘
            %i> not
            ’, as a clue that we're shifting to a metalanguage, or at least merely remaining agnostic rather than outright disagreeing.
          %p
            The distinction between object language and metalanguage exists even in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a statement which is neither provable nor disprovable (like the continuum hypothesis), in classical mathematics it is still provable that “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is either true or false.” Moreover, classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+theory"} model theory
            often restricts itself to
            %span.newWikiWord
              two-valued models
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/two-valued+model"} ?
            in which the only truth values are “true” and “false,” although classical logic still holds in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra"} Boolean-valued
            models, and in such a case the truth value of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            may be neither “true” nor “false,” although the truth value of “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            or not
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          %h2#prehistory Prehistory
          %p In
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"}> Georg Hegel
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phenomenology+of+Spirit"} Phenomenology of Spirit
              (1807)
          %p
            the following comment about mathematical proof (in section
            %em
              %a{:href => "https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/hegel/works/ph/phprefac.htm#12"} 12 Historical and mathematical proof
            of the Preface) might be read as being a complaint about the traditional non-constructive concept of proof and about the traditional lack of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+relevance"} proof relevance
          %blockquote
            %p#PoSOnProofRelevance All the same, while proof is essential in the case of mathematical knowledge, it still does not have the significance and nature of being a moment in the result itself; the proof is over when we get the result, and has disappeared. The process of mathematical proof does not belong to the object; it is a function that takes place outside the matter in hand.
            %p
              %a{:href => "https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/hegel/help/finpref.htm#m042"}> footnote 42
              \: Mathematical truths are not thought to be known unless proved true. Their demonstrations are not, however, kept as parts of what they prove, but are only our subjective means towards knowing the latter. In philosophy, however, consequences always form part of the essence made manifest in them, which returns to itself in such expressions.
          %p
            See also earlier conceptions of proofs expressing the ‘cause’ of a theorem, where
            %em reductio
            proofs in particular were taken generally to fail. Such an idea goes back to Aristotle for whom a proper answer to the question “Why is the angle in a semicircle a right-angle?” gives its cause.
          %ul
            %li
              Paolo Mancosu,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Philosophy of Mathematics and Mathematical Practice in the Seventeenth Century
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
